# SPEC 11.3: a `context` declaration is a *module-local* name, and the type it
# generates has no name at all. Two modules that each declare `context Fixture`
# declare two different types with two different constructors, and a program
# holding both has to be able to call each of them.
#
# It could not. `monomorphize::name_of` mangled a context constructor as
# `ctx$<name>` with nothing in front of it, so both landed on `ctx$Fixture` and
# one definition answered to the other's calls. The symptom was not a wrong
# answer but a spectacular one: the inliner empties the smaller of the two
# constructors into its callers, `dce` marks the emptied one unbuilt, the
# backend emits an *abort* under the shared symbol, and the other module's every
# test dies on "this function was never built".
#
# Two shapes, because the two ways a program comes to hold both declarations are
# different and only one of them was ever reported:
#
#   * `//lib/app` — one suite, one binary, two `testing` surfaces that each
#     export a `Fixture`. Both backends built this wrong.
#   * `//lib/writer` + `//lib/reader` — two packages, each declaring its own
#     `Fixture` in its own test source, batched into one test binary by
#     `commands/test.rs`'s `run_batch`. Native only, because `--output=` does
#     not batch — which is exactly why this one hid: each suite alone passed,
#     JavaScript passed, and a *second* run of the failing command passed too,
#     since the suite that did pass is cached by then and one fresh suite does
#     not batch. A cold CI saw red; a developer running it twice never did.
#
# Every native run below is cold — the harness copies `repo/` into a fresh
# scratch tree and no step before it built these targets — and the `--output=js`
# run beside each is the cross-backend row: the same suites, the same summary,
# on the backend that defines the language and on the one the host links.
#
# Schema: the repository-case manifest, cli/tests/harness/case.rs. Textproto,
# but not a Buri file: only repo/ below is a Buri repository.

doc: "two modules that declare a context under one name are two contexts, and each caller reaches its own"

# One binary holding both declarations, built natively from cold.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/app"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "app.txt"
  stream: OUT
}

# The same suite on the reference backend. One golden for both, so the row is
# the file rather than a sentence about it.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/app", "--force", "--output=js"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "app.txt"
  stream: OUT
}

# Two suites, one batched test binary, cold. This is the reported shape.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/writer", "//lib/reader"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "batched.txt"
  stream: OUT
}

# And the same two suites on the reference backend, which compiles them one at
# a time and must agree about every verdict.
run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/writer", "//lib/reader", "--force", "--output=js"]
  exit: 0
  golden: "batched.txt"
  stream: OUT
}

# The negative twin: one assertion made wrong, so that a green batch above is
# evidence rather than an assertion about one. On `--output=js` because the
# report a *native* failure prints is the exit status and a message rather than
# the runner's two values — `failure_format`'s manifest is the argument.
edit {
  file: "lib/writer/test/writer.buri"
  replace: "assert.eq(written(ctx, 1), 1);"
  with: "assert.eq(written(ctx, 1), 2);"
}

run {
  args: ["test", "//lib/writer", "//lib/reader", "--force", "--output=js"]
  exit: 1
  golden: "batched_failure.txt"
  stream: OUT
}
